      "semantic": {
        "text": "ModernWallet Free Financial Calculators Calculators All calculators Auto Loan Mortgage Retirement Investing Portfolio Real Estate Net Worth Budget Tax Resolution Estate Planning Probate Elder Care Trump Account 529 College Savings Coast FIRE Business Loan Payoff Personal Loan Interest Per Day Taxable vs. Tax-Deferred Credit Card Payoff IUL Merchant Cash Advance Invoice Factoring Business Line of Credit PTO Cash-Out Zakat Life Insurance Needs Self-Employment Tax Operating Cash Flow Mileage Deduction Cash Conversion Cycle Freelance Rate HELOC S-Corp Tax Commercial Mortgage CD Calculator Emergency Fund Debt Consolidation Inflation Expense Ratio Social Security Break-Even Mileage Reimbursement Portfolio Rebalancing Hurricane Deductible Annuity Credit Card Interest Loan Payment HYSA vs CD Trump Account vs 529 Mortgage Recast Social Security COLA Biweekly Mortgage Roth vs Traditional 401(k) Pay Off Mortgage or Invest Florida Amendment 3 Student Loan Garnishment Tax Bracket Debt-to-Income Ratio Taxable Social Security Checklists Charitable Donation Deduction Salary Car Loan Interest Deduction Paycheck Rent vs Buy Stock Capital Gains Tax Hidden Cost of Owning a Home × ModernWallet / Compare / QuickBooks Alternatives QuickBooks Alternatives 2026: Prices, Plans and Limits Compared By Jonathan Velez , Personal Finance Writer & Editor · Reviewed against primary sources · Updated November 13, 2026 The main QuickBooks alternatives are FreshBooks, Xero, Zoho Books, Wave and Zoho Invoice, and the right pick depends on how many people need a login, how many clients or invoices you bill, and whether your accountant needs to work in the same file. This page covers QuickBooks alternatives for small businesses and freelancers, using each company's published prices. The subject is QuickBooks Online, which Intuit sells in five tiers, from a free plan to a $340 a month Advanced plan. The alternatives price differently: FreshBooks by billable clients, Xero and Zoho Books by plan tier, and Wave by card-payment fees on its free plan. The page lists what each provider publishes. For context on the wider category, see our roundup of accounting software for freelancers and the side-by-side comparison hub . If you are choosing between only the two best-known options, our QuickBooks vs FreshBooks head-to-head prices them by users and billable clients. QuickBooks and alternatives: published prices Prices from each provider's own website, checked November 13, 2026. Regular prices are shown; first-months promotions are noted separately and change often. Product Fees and pricing Best fit QuickBooks Online Free plan ($0, 1 user, 2 invoices a month); Simple Start $38/mo (1 user); Essentials $85/mo (3 users); Plus $140/mo (5 users); Advanced $340/mo (25 users). Regular prices; 50% off for the first 3 months is advertised for new paid plans Fits businesses that want a widely used platform their accountant likely already knows, with tiers up to 25 users FreshBooks Lite $23/mo (5 billable clients); Plus $43/mo (50 clients); Premium $70/mo (unlimited clients); Select is quote-based with no public price. Team members $11/mo each as an add-on. Regular prices; 80% off for the first 4 months is advertised Fits freelancers and service businesses whose cost should follow client count Xero Early $27/mo (20 invoices and quotes, 5 bills); Growing $59/mo; Established $97/mo. Regular prices; 80% off for the first 3 months is advertised for new US customers Fits small businesses that want bank reconciliation and forecasting on every plan Zoho Books Free $0 (1 user plus 1 accountant, 1,000 invoices a year); Standard $20/mo ($15 billed annually, 3 users); Professional $50/mo ($40 annually, 5 users); Premium $70/mo ($60 annually, 10 users); Elite $150/mo ($120 annually, 10 users); Ultimate $275/mo ($240 annually, 15 users) Fits small businesses that want a free entry plan and a long ladder of paid tiers Wave Starter $0 (card payments 2.9% + $0.60 per transaction; American Express 3.4% + $0.60); Pro $19/mo or $190/yr; payroll and bookkeeping are paid add-ons Fits very small businesses and freelancers that need basic invoicing and bookkeeping Zoho Invoice Free: up to 2 users, 3 projects and 500 invoices a year; no paid tier is published Fits freelancers that only need invoicing, not full accounting Are you one of these vendors? Update your listing How to Choose a QuickBooks Alternative Start with the billing unit, because it decides how the price grows. QuickBooks charges by plan tier, and each tier caps the number of users: one on Simple Start, three on Essentials, five on Plus and 25 on Advanced. FreshBooks charges by billable clients, so few clients with many invoices cost little, while hundreds of small clients move you to the top plan. Xero and Zoho Books charge by tier, and Zoho Books also publishes user and invoice limits on every tier. Wave charges nothing for software on Starter and earns its fee from card payments. Next, count the people who need a login. A two-person firm fits QuickBooks Essentials, Zoho Books Standard, or a FreshBooks plan plus a paid team-member seat. Check whether your bookkeeper or accountant is counted as a user or has a separate free seat, since QuickBooks lists two accountant seats on its lower plans and Zoho Books includes one accountant on its free plan. Then check what the entry plan limits. Xero Early caps invoices and quotes at 20 and bills at 5. QuickBooks Free allows 2 invoices a month. Zoho Invoice allows 500 invoices a year. A cap that looks generous at 10 clients can bind at 100. Finally, separate subscription prices from payment fees and add-ons. Card processing, Automated Clearing House (ACH) bank transfers, payroll and extra users are priced apart from the monthly plan. Wave payroll, FreshBooks payroll at $40 a month plus $6 per user, and Xero payroll at $36 a month plus $6 per person all sit on top of the plan. A short checklist: How many people need to log in, and is the accountant counted? How many invoices, bills and clients do you handle in a busy month? Do you need inventory, project profitability or payroll, and what does each cost? What do card and bank payments cost on top of the plan? Can your accountant work in the product, or will they ask for an export? Promotional prices last 3 or 4 months and then revert, so compare regular prices. For a longer view of the category, the invoicing apps roundup lists more tools priced by the same units. Want to be added to this alternatives page? Contact us FreshBooks FreshBooks is an invoicing-first accounting product. It publishes four plans: Lite at $23 a month for 5 billable clients, Plus at $43 for 50, Premium at $70 for unlimited clients, and Select, which is quote-based with no public price. Lite covers invoices to 5 clients, expense tracking, estimates and tax-time reports. Plus adds 50 clients, proposals, retainers, financial and accounting reports, receipt scanning and accountant access. Premium adds unlimited clients, bill receipt scanning and project profitability. Team members cost $11 a month each, payroll is $40 a month plus $6 per user, and FreshBooks advertises 80% off for the first four months. It fits freelancers and service firms with a modest client list, where billable clients are easy to count and invoicing is the main task. Versus QuickBooks, FreshBooks' pricing page lists no inventory tracking and prices around clients rather than users, so many small customers can reach the top plan quickly. QuickBooks Plus lists inventory tracking and a five-user limit. Worked example: a two-person consulting firm with 30 clients needs Plus at $43 a month, or $516 a year, plus one extra team member at $11 a month, or $132, for $648 a year at regular prices. The same firm on QuickBooks Essentials, which includes three users, pays $85 a month, or $1,020 a year. Xero Xero is an accounting platform sold in three US plans: Early at $27 a month, Growing at $59 and Established at $97. Early limits invoices and quotes to 20 and bills to 5, and lists bank reconciliation, sales tax, real-time reports, a 30-day cash flow forecast and free ACH bill payments. Growing lists no invoice or bill limit and adds a 60-day forecast, customizable dashboards and budgets. Established adds multiple currencies, project time and cost tracking, expense claims and a 180-day forecast. Xero advertises 80% off the first three months for new US customers. It fits small businesses with several bank accounts to reconcile, teams that want forecasting, and businesses that invoice in more than one currency. Versus QuickBooks, Xero's page does not list a user limit by plan, so seat rules are not stated by plan. Its entry plan is tighter on document counts than QuickBooks Simple Start, and payroll is an add-on at $36 a month plus $6 per employee or contractor. Worked example: a two-person firm that sends about 40 invoices a month needs Growing at $59 a month, or $708 a year at regular prices, since Early would cap at 20. Add payroll for one employee and the add-on is $42 a month, or $504 a year, bringing the total to $1,212. Zoho Books Zoho Books has the longest published ladder of the group. The Free plan covers one user plus one accountant and 1,000 invoices a year. Standard is $20 a month, or $15 billed annually, for 3 users and 5,000 invoices a year. Professional is $50 ($40 annually) for 5 users, and adds purchase orders, multi-currency, project profitability, retainers and inventory. Premium is $70 ($60), Elite $150 ($120) and Ultimate $275 ($240). Extra users cost $3 a month, or $2.50 billed annually. Higher limits are available by contacting Zoho. It fits small businesses that want a free start and prefer to move up one step at a time as they add users, inventory or reporting. Versus QuickBooks, Zoho Books lists lower prices at each step, but fewer small-business accountants may already work in it, which matters if you share files. QuickBooks Advanced lists 25 users, while Zoho Books tops out at 15 on Ultimate. Worked example: a two-person firm on Standard pays $15 a month billed annually, or $180 a year, or $240 a year if billed monthly. If the firm needs inventory, Professional costs $40 a month annually, or $480 a year. Wave Wave's Starter plan costs $0 and lists unlimited invoices, estimates, bills and bookkeeping records, with online payments as an option. Card payments on Starter cost 2.9% + $0.60 per transaction, and American Express costs 3.4% + $0.60. Pro costs $19 a month or $190 a year and adds multi-user access, invoice attachments and removal of Wave branding. Unlimited receipt capture is a paid add-on at $8 a month on Pro. Payroll is an add-on listed from $25 a month or $40 a month, and Wave Advisors, a done-for-you bookkeeping service, starts at $149 a month. It fits solo owners and very small businesses with straightforward books who want software at no subscription cost. Versus QuickBooks, Wave's pricing page lists no inventory or project profitability features, and its income comes from payment fees. On Pro, the first 10 card payments each month cost 2.9% + $0; later ones cost 2.9% + $0.60. Worked example: a two-person firm on Pro pays $190 a year. If it collects $60,000 a year by card on Starter, the fees are 2.9% of $60,000, or $1,740, plus $0.60 on each of, say, 600 payments, or $360, for $2,100 before any plan fee. Payment fees can outweigh the subscription difference, so the payment mix matters more than the plan price. Free QuickBooks Alternatives: Zoho Invoice Zoho Invoice is a free invoicing product, not a full accounting system. Its page lists limits of two users, three projects and 500 invoices a year, up to two organizations per account, and Zoho Invoice branding on invoices. It publishes no paid tier; businesses that outgrow it are pointed to support or to other Zoho products. It fits freelancers who send a modest number of invoices, track a few projects, and keep their books elsewhere or with an accountant. Versus QuickBooks, it has no general ledger focus and no paid upgrade path inside the product. QuickBooks Free allows 2 invoices a month, which is 24 a year, so Zoho Invoice's 500 a year is far higher for pure invoicing. Worked example: a freelancer sending 15 invoices a month sends 180 a year, well inside the 500 cap, so the annual cost is $0. Sending 50 a month reaches 600 and passes the cap in the tenth month. Who This Is Not For This page is a price and limits list. It is not a fit for several kinds of reader. Businesses that need a recommendation for their own tax or filing situation. Prices and plan limits are public facts, but which tool suits a given entity type, state or filing status is a question for an accountant. Companies with complex payroll, multi-entity consolidation or heavy inventory across warehouses. Only the higher tiers cover these, and a pricing table cannot show how well each handles them. The business bank accounts roundup covers a different part of the stack. Readers who want a verdict on which product has the best software. These pages list published prices, not results from using the products. Businesses already in the middle of a tax year with a working setup. Moving mid-year means exporting history and re-mapping accounts, a cost no price table includes. If you are a solo earner trying to understand what you will owe rather than which tool to buy, the 1099 tax calculator is a better starting point. What Would Change Our Answer About QuickBooks Alternatives The ranking of prices on this page can change when a provider changes a plan. A new plan tier, a change in user limits or an end to a free plan would change which product lists the lowest price for a given team size. Xero, FreshBooks and QuickBooks all run first-months promotions, so a longer promotion would lower the first-year cost without changing regular prices. Five facts would change a pick for a given reader. A user count above the plan cap. QuickBooks Essentials includes three users and Plus five, so a sixth person moves a team to Advanced at $340 a month. A client count above 50, which moves FreshBooks from Plus to Premium. Card volume. Heavy card collection can make Wave's per-transaction fees the largest line, not the plan. An accountant who only works in one product, which can outweigh a price gap. A need for inventory, which QuickBooks lists on Plus and Zoho Books lists on Professional. Prices on this page were checked on November 13, 2026 and were checked on that date. Tax treatment is outside this page; the guide to setting aside money for taxes explains how self-employed earners estimate it. When QuickBooks Is Still the Better Fit QuickBooks fits businesses whose accountant or bookkeeper already works in it, since sharing one file avoids exports and re-mapping. It also fits teams that need its higher tiers: Plus lists inventory tracking, project profitability and five users, and Advanced lists 25 users, custom user permissions, automated workflows and batch invoices. It fits teams that prefer a ladder with no annual contract. Paid plans carry a 30-day free trial and can be canceled at any time. The cost is the price: Simple Start at $38 a month lists one user, and the regular price of Essentials is $85 a month. A solo owner who only sends invoices can pay far less with Wave, Zoho Books Free or Zoho Invoice. If you already use QuickBooks and pay an accountant to maintain it, the switch often costs more than it saves, because data export, account mapping and retraining take paid time. Verdict Each product above publishes its prices on its own site. QuickBooks lists the highest regular tier prices of the group, FreshBooks prices by client count, Xero and Zoho Books price by tier, and Wave, Zoho Books and Zoho Invoice publish free entry plans with limits. For QuickBooks alternatives, the next step is to count your users, clients and monthly invoices, then compare those numbers with the limits in the table. Frequently asked questions W